{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"2498","artifact_type":"architect","name":"plan/architect-backlog-core-decompose.md@sha256:1cfca3cf44d6b75783bdc8bb4591384d474874819c0fc605590e2ca8fbd597ab"},"owner_reference":"2498","content":"# Architecture Spec \u2014 Decompose `backlog_core` operations.py and server.py\n\nItem: #2498 \u00b7 Phase 3 (architecture, WHAT to build) \u00b7 Produced 2026-09-03\n\nThis spec defines interfaces, contracts, module boundaries, and the re-export\nstub structure for decomposing `backlog_core/operations.py` (5407 lines) and\n`backlog_core/server.py` (4640 lines) into cohesive sibling modules. It does\nnot implement \u2014 it specifies exact signatures, exact import blocks, and the\none behavioural change (the dual-output contract fix) precisely enough that a\ntask-worker agent can execute each phase without re-deriving a design\ndecision.\n\nAll line numbers below are verified against `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/{operations,server}.py` at the current HEAD (2026-09-03).\n\n## 0. Scope and non-negotiables (carried from Phase 1/2, restated for the implementer)\n\n- No behaviour change except the one documented in \u00a75 (dual-output fix).\n- No new `pyproject.toml` beside the modules. No collapse to one file.\n- Module names are **plain**, not underscore-prefixed: `section_filter.py`,\n  `view_assembly.py`, `view_budget.py`. This follows the `sam_schema/__init__.py`\n  precedent (`sam_schema.core.dependencies`, `sam_schema.core.models` \u2014 plain\n  submodule names), not the `backlog_core/__init__.py` `_branch_delegates.py`\n  precedent. See \u00a77 Concerns for why the codebase-analysis artifact's\n  underscore recommendation is rejected.\n- No `@mcp.tool()`-decorated function leaves `server.py`.\n- Every symbol importable today from `backlog_core.operations` or\n  `backlog_core.server` remains importable from that same path after the move\n  (verified against the Consumers/Other-References sections of #2498 in\n  \u00a76 below \u2014 this is a pinned contract, not an assumption).\n- `from __future__ import annotations` first import in every new module.\n  Full return-type annotations on every function, matching the existing style\n  in `operations.py`/`server.py`.\n\n## 1. Phase 0 \u2014 ADR\n\n**File:** `docs/adr/backlog-core-is-a-package.md` (path as assigned).\n\nContent: transcribe the \"Decision to record as an ADR\" section already on\nbacklog item #2498 verbatim (package-vs-launcher facts, verified during PR\n#2496) \u2014 no new content to draft, this is a copy-to-durable-location task.\nUse this repo's existing ADR frontmatter style (status, date, context,\ndecision, consequences) to match the two ADR conventions already present in\nthe codebase (see \u00a77 concern \u2014 the exact directory is flagged there, use\n`docs/adr/backlog-core-is-a-package.md` as instructed unless the concern is\nresolved before implementation starts).\n\nAcceptance: `.claude/rules/` is NOT used for this (repo policy: durable\narchitecture decisions belong in `docs/`, not `.claude/rules/`, which is for\ncurrent-requirement-only agent instructions).\n\n## 2. Dependency graph (why the phase order matters)\n\n```\noperations.py (core, retains ~5150 LOC of non-view logic)\n     ^                                  ^\n     | re-export (bottom-of-file)       | re-export (bottom-of-file)\n     |                                  |\nsection_filter.py  <\u2500\u2500\u2500imports\u2500\u2500\u2500  view_assembly.py\n     ^  (shared helpers,                |\n     |   see \u00a73.2, \u00a74.2)             |\n     \u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                                         \nserver.py (core: @mcp.tool handlers + non-view helpers)\n     ^\n     | re-export (bottom-of-file)\n     |\nview_budget.py\n```\n\n- `view_assembly.py` imports from `section_filter.py` \u2014 one-directional,\n  confirmed no reverse dependency (view_assembly's targets are strictly\n  downstream callers of section_filter's targets; grep confirms\n  `section_filter.py`'s five functions never call any of `view_assembly.py`'s\n  five).\n- `section_filter.py` and `view_assembly.py` each import a small number of\n  helper functions **back** from `operations.py` core (\u00a73.2, \u00a74.2) \u2014 this is a\n  genuine, unavoidable shared-dependency, not a design flaw, but it means\n  `operations.py`'s re-export block (`from .section_filter import ...`,\n  `from .view_assembly import ...`) **must be placed at the bottom of the\n  file, after all of operations.py's own remaining top-level definitions**.\n  Placing it at the top (the naive re-export-stub placement suggested by the\n  codebase-analysis artifact's `backlog_core/__init__.py` precedent) creates a\n  circular import: `operations` \u2192 `section_filter` \u2192 `operations` (for\n  `_slice_body_by_header_indices` etc.) resolving to a partially-initialized\n  module before those names exist. Bottom-of-file placement is the standard,\n  minimal fix \u2014 no module needs to be split further to avoid it.\n- `view_budget.py` has the identical relationship with `server.py` (\u00a76.4):\n  one helper (`_sections_index_from_result`) must stay behind in `server.py`\n  and be imported back by `view_budget.py`. Same bottom-of-file placement\n  rule applies to `server.py`'s re-export block.\n\n## 3. Phase 1 \u2014 `backlog_core/section_filter.py`\n\n### 3.1 Functions moved (exact signatures, unchanged)\n\n```python\ndef _build_sections_index_from_body(body: str) -> str: ...\ndef _filter_sections(item: BacklogItem, section: str) -> dict[str, Section | GroomedData]: ...\ndef _build_sections_metadata(\n    body: str, show: str | int | None, since: str | None, section: str | None = None\n) -> dict[str, SectionEntryMetadata | GroomedSectionMetadata]: ...\ndef _build_sections_compact(body: str) -> list[dict[str, str | int]]: ...\ndef _apply_body_section_filter(result: ViewItemResult, body: str, section: str) -> str: ...  # contract changes, see \u00a75\n```\n\nSource lines (pre-move): 2024, 2170, 2297, 2367, 2634 in `operations.py`.\n\n### 3.2 Imports `section_filter.py` needs\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom .models import BacklogItem, GroomedData, GroomedSectionMetadata, Section, SectionEntryMetadata, ViewItemResult\nfrom .parsing import split_body_sections\nfrom .operations import _int_field, _compact_entry_count, _merge_section_entries, _resolve_section_indices, _section_display_title, _slice_body_by_header_indices\n```\n\nConfirm the exact `.models` import names against the live symbols already\nimported at the top of `operations.py` \u2014 do not re-derive types, copy the\nexisting import line's names for the types this module's five functions\nreference.\n\n**Note on the `from .operations import ...` line**: `_int_field` (2554) and\n`_compact_entry_count` (2571) are used exclusively inside `_build_sections_compact`\n(a function moving to this module) \u2014 they have no other caller in\n`operations.py`. `_resolve_section_indices` (2086) is used exclusively by\n`_filter_sections`, `_apply_body_section_filter`, and `view_assembly.py`'s\n`_assemble_view_compact` \u2014 all three callers are moving out of `operations.py`.\nLeft in place, these three functions become dead weight in `operations.py`\nthat exists only to be imported back \u2014 see \u00a77 Concerns for the recommendation\nto move them too. This spec keeps them in `operations.py` per the explicit\ninstruction not to re-derive module assignments; the import above is required\neither way.\n\n`_merge_section_entries` (1995), `_section_display_title` (2007), and\n`_slice_body_by_header_indices` (2605) are genuinely shared: each has at\nleast one caller that is *not* moving (`_merge_section_entries` is also\ncalled from `view_assembly.py`'s `_build_sections_from_yaml_item`;\n`_section_display_title` is also called from `_render_section_index`, which\nstays in `operations.py`; `_slice_body_by_header_indices` is also called from\n`narrow_body_to_named_sections`, which stays in `operations.py`). These three\ncannot move without duplicating logic and must be imported back regardless of\nhow the orphan question in \u00a77 is resolved.\n\n### 3.3 Contract note \u2014 `_filter_sections`, `_build_sections_metadata`, `_build_sections_compact`\n\nNo contract changes. Pure functions (no `result` mutation), signatures\nunchanged, docstrings copied verbatim.\n\n## 4. Phase 2 \u2014 `backlog_core/view_assembly.py`\n\n### 4.1 Functions moved (exact signatures, unchanged)\n\n```python\ndef _build_sections_from_yaml_item(item: BacklogItem) -> dict[str, SectionEntryMetadata | GroomedSectionMetadata]: ...\ndef _paginate_body_result(result: ViewItemResult, body: str, offset: int, limit: int) -> None: ...\ndef _populate_yaml_item_content(result: ViewItemResult, item: BacklogItem, section: str | None) -> None: ...\ndef _assemble_view_compact(\n    result: ViewItemResult, item: BacklogItem | None, body: str, section: str | None = None\n) -> None: ...\ndef _assemble_view_content(\n    result: ViewItemResult,\n    item: BacklogItem | None,\n    *,\n    include_content: bool,\n    section: str | None,\n    show: str | int | None,\n    since: str | None,\n    offset: int,\n    limit: int,\n) -> None:\n    \"\"\"Populate *result* with body/sections for ``view_item``.\n\n    Mutates *result* in place \u2014 sets ``body``, ``sections``, or\n    ``sections_metadata`` depending on *include_content*. Always sets\n    ``sections_index`` when *include_content* is ``False`` and the item has\n    structured sections, so callers can discover available sections without\n    loading the full body.\n    \"\"\"\n```\n\nSource lines (pre-move): 2234, 2472, 2531, 2712, 2821 in `operations.py`. Signature\nand docstring verified directly against `operations.py:2821-2839` \u2014 there is\nno `paginate` parameter; `paginate` is derived locally inside the function\nbody from `offset`/`limit` (confirmed by reading the live source, not\nreconstructed from the call-site grep).\n\n### 4.2 Imports `view_assembly.py` needs\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom .models import BacklogItem, GroomedSectionMetadata, SectionEntryMetadata, ViewItemResult\nfrom .section_filter import _apply_body_section_filter, _build_sections_index_from_body, _build_sections_metadata\nfrom .operations import _entry_owning_headers, _merge_section_entries, _render_paged_entry_body, _render_section_index, _sections_from_body_or_yaml\n```\n\n**Note on the `from .operations import ...` line**: `_entry_owning_headers`\n(2396), `_render_paged_entry_body` (2442), and `_sections_from_body_or_yaml`\n(2766) are used exclusively by functions moving to this module (`_paginate_body_result`\nand `_assemble_view_content` respectively) \u2014 same orphan pattern as \u00a73.2, see\n\u00a77. `_merge_section_entries` and `_render_section_index` are genuinely shared\nper \u00a73.2 and stay in `operations.py`.\n\n### 4.3 Cross-module note\n\n`_assemble_view_content` calls `_apply_body_section_filter` and\n`_build_sections_metadata` (both now in `section_filter.py`) and\n`_build_sections_index_from_body` (also `section_filter.py`) \u2014 confirming the\none-directional `view_assembly.py \u2192 section_filter.py` edge in \u00a72.\n\n## 5. The dual-output contract fix (`_apply_body_section_filter`)\n\n**Chosen contract: pure-function (`-> str`), caller owns the assignment to\n`result.body`.**\n\nRejected alternative (pure-mutation, `-> None`): the sole caller (operations.py:2872, `_assemble_view_content`) uses the **local** `body` variable\nreturned by this call in three subsequent branches (feeding\n`_build_sections_metadata(body, ...)`, `_sections_from_body_or_yaml(body, ...)`,\nand `_build_sections_index_from_body(body)`) before `result.body` is ever\nread again. A pure-mutation contract would force rewriting all three of those\ndownstream reads from `body` to `result.body`, a larger and riskier diff for\nno behavioural benefit. Pure-function is the smaller, correct change.\n\n### 5.1 Exact change to `_apply_body_section_filter` (moving to `section_filter.py`)\n\nRemove the line `result.body = body` immediately before `return body`\n(operations.py:2668 in the pre-move source). The function keeps setting\n`result.section_filter_miss` / `result.section_filter_valid_names` on the\nmiss branch \u2014 those are not part of the dual-output defect (they are\ngenuinely `result`-scoped signals with no return-value duplicate) and are\nunaffected.\n\n```python\ndef _apply_body_section_filter(result: ViewItemResult, body: str, section: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Narrow *body* to the requested section(s).\n\n    ... (docstring otherwise unchanged; drop the \"and result.body\" clause\n    from the summary line and the Returns section, since result.body is no\n    longer mutated by this function \u2014 the caller assigns it) ...\n    \"\"\"\n    spans = split_body_sections(body)\n    names = [span.name for span in spans]\n    matched = _resolve_section_indices(names, section)\n    if matched:\n        body = _slice_body_by_header_indices(body, spans, matched)\n    else:\n        result.section_filter_miss = True\n        result.section_filter_valid_names = names\n    return body\n```\n\n### 5.2 Exact change to the one caller (`_assemble_view_content`, moving to `view_assembly.py`)\n\nAt the call site (operations.py:2872 pre-move, inside the `if section is not None:` branch):\n\n```python\n                body = _apply_body_section_filter(result, body, section)\n                result.body = body          # <-- ADD this line: caller now owns the assignment\n                if result.section_filter_miss:\n```\n\nThis is the only caller in the codebase (confirmed by the backlog item's\nverification correction: 1 caller at line 2872, not the originally-cited 3).\nConfirm with a fresh grep for `_apply_body_section_filter(` across\n`backlog_core/` immediately before implementing \u2014 the item's own concern log\nalready flags this count as having drifted once; re-verify at execution\ntime, not from this spec.\n\n### 5.3 Test impact\n\n`backlog_core/tests/test_apply_body_section_filter_forms.py`,\n`test_section_filter_miss.py`, `test_view_section_form_resolution.py`,\n`test_view_sections_metadata_sync.py`, and\n`test_section_boundary_scanner_live_3152.py` all import\n`_apply_body_section_filter` directly and call it standalone (not through\n`view_item`). Any assertion in those tests that reads `result.body`\n**immediately after** calling `_apply_body_section_filter` without also\nreading the function's return value will now see the pre-call value of\n`result.body` (since the function no longer mutates it) instead of the\nnarrowed body. Per the \"no test modified to accommodate the new structure\"\noutcome in the feature-context doc, this must be treated as a genuine\nregression signal, not waved through \u2014 read each of these five test files'\nassertions before/after this change and confirm each one either (a) already\nasserts on the return value, or (b) needs updating because it was relying on\nthe old dual-write, in which case document that explicitly as the one\naccepted test change (the feature-context doc's outcome #6 already\nanticipates this: \"the dual-output contract ... is resolved ... rather than\ncarried forward\" is an *intended* behavioural change, so a test asserting the\n*old*, buggy dual-write behavior is a test asserting the bug and updating it\nis correct, not a regression).\n\n## 6. Phase 3 \u2014 `backlog_core/view_budget.py`\n\n### 6.1 Symbols moved (exact signatures, unchanged)\n\n```python\n_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET: Final[int] = 4_000  # constant, was untyped module global at server.py:204\n_enc: tiktoken.Encoding = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")\n\ndef _token_count(serialised: str) -> int: ...\ndef _filter_view_sections(\n    response: dict[str, object], sections: list[str], result: _models.ViewItemResult\n) -> dict[str, object]: ...\ndef _build_compact_manifest(\n    result: _models.ViewItemResult, full_response: dict[str, object], selector: str\n) -> dict[str, object]: ...\ndef _build_over_budget_view(result: _models.ViewItemResult, full_chars: int, selector: str) -> dict[str, object]: ...\n```\n\nSource lines (pre-move): 204 (`_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`), 209 (`_token_count`), 842\n(`_filter_view_sections`), 1886 (`_build_compact_manifest`), 1963\n(`_build_over_budget_view`) in `server.py`. `_enc` (the tiktoken encoder\ninstance backing `_token_count`) moves alongside `_token_count` \u2014 it is not\nindependently listed in the target set but has no separable meaning apart\nfrom the function it backs.\n\n### 6.2 Imports `view_budget.py` needs\n\n```python\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nfrom typing import Final\n\nimport tiktoken\n\nfrom . import models as _models\nfrom .server import _sections_index_from_result  # see \u00a76.4 \u2014 genuine shared dependency, imported back\n```\n\n### 6.3 Critical: `server.py` MUST re-import all five moved names into its own module namespace, not merely reference `view_budget.*` internally\n\nUnlike the Phase 1/2 re-export (which exists to keep `backlog_core.operations`\nimportable for *external* consumers), `server.py`'s re-export block for this\nphase has a second, load-bearing purpose: multiple call sites \u2014 both internal\nto `server.py` and in tests \u2014 reference these names as **bare identifiers or\nas `server.<name>` attributes**, not via a qualified `view_budget.<name>`\npath:\n\n- Internal, non-view callers that stay in `server.py` and must keep working\n  unchanged: `_view_payload_token_count` (server.py:221, calls `_token_count`),\n  a match-context sizing call at server.py:648 (`_token_count`), and a\n  `_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET` pagination check at server.py:1582 (`_token_count`) \u2014\n  **`_token_count` is a general JSON-token-counting utility, not exclusively\n  a view-path concern**, despite moving to `view_budget.py` per the explicit\n  target list (see \u00a77 Concerns).\n- Test files patch and import by exact dotted path:\n  `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py` does\n  `mocker.patch(\"backlog_core.server._enc.encode\", return_value=...)` (5\n  call sites) and `from backlog_core.server import _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` /\n  `from backlog_core.server import _build_over_budget_view` (multiple sites).\n  `backlog_core/tests/test_view_over_budget_gate.py` and\n  `test_view_pagination_section_index.py` access `server._token_count`,\n  `server._VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET`, `server._filter_view_sections` as attributes\n  on an imported `server` module object.\n\n**Required fix:** add to `server.py`, replacing the original definitions in\nplace, a single import block:\n\n```python\nfrom .view_budget import (\n    _VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET,\n    _build_compact_manifest,\n    _build_over_budget_view,\n    _enc,\n    _filter_view_sections,\n    _token_count,\n)\n```\n\nBecause these are plain module-level imports (not a re-export-only stub),\nevery existing bare-name call site inside `server.py` (\u00a7 list above) and\nevery existing `server.<name>` / `mocker.patch(\"backlog_core.server.<name>...\")`\nreference in tests keeps working with **zero test changes**, since Python\nbinds the imported names as ordinary attributes of the `server` module. This\nis the same mechanism as a Phase 1/2 re-export stub, just serving a dual\npurpose (external importability *and* internal bare-name use) instead of one.\n\n### 6.4 The one helper that must stay behind: `_sections_index_from_result`\n\n`_sections_index_from_result` (server.py:1928) sits physically between\n`_build_compact_manifest` (1886) and `_build_over_budget_view` (1963) and is\ncalled by both \u2014 but it is not in the assigned target list, so per this\nspec's non-negotiables it stays in `server.py`. `view_budget.py` imports it\nback (\u00a76.2), which \u2014 combined with `server.py`'s re-export of the five moved\nnames (\u00a76.3) \u2014 creates the same bottom-of-file-placement requirement as \u00a72:\n`server.py`'s `from .view_budget import (...)` block must be placed after\n`_sections_index_from_result`'s definition in the file, not before it, to\navoid a circular-import failure at module load. `_sections_index_from_result`\nis also directly imported by tests\n(`plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_server.py`,\n`from backlog_core.server import _sections_index_from_result`) \u2014 it stays\nimportable from `server.py` with no change needed there, since it never\nmoved.\n\n### 6.5 `tests/conftest.py` docstring \u2014 must be updated, not left stale\n\n`backlog_core/tests/conftest.py`'s module docstring currently states:\n\n> Importing `backlog_core.server` initializes\n> `server._enc = tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")` at module load\n\nAfter this move, `_enc` is initialized in `view_budget.py` and reaches\n`server._enc` only via the re-export import in \u00a76.3 (still true at import\ntime \u2014 importing `backlog_core.server` still transitively triggers\n`tiktoken.get_encoding(\"cl100k_base\")`, just one hop further down the import\nchain through `view_budget.py`). The pre-warming behaviour this conftest\nimplements is unaffected (it seeds `tiktoken.registry.ENCODINGS` globally,\nnot a specific module attribute), so no functional test change is needed \u2014\nbut the docstring's factual claim about *which module* performs the\ninitialization becomes stale and must be corrected as part of this phase\n(one-line docstring edit: `server._enc` \u2192 `view_budget._enc (re-exported as\nserver._enc)`, or equivalent wording). This is in scope for this phase, not\na follow-up: leaving a docstring asserting an inaccurate import-chain fact\nabout the exact defect class this refactor exists to prevent (stale\ndocumentation of the codebase's own structure) is the kind of drift the\nfeature-context document's outcome #1 (locate code by module name without\nguessing) exists to eliminate.\n\n## 7. Concerns\n\n<concerns>\n\n**1. Underscore-module-naming recommendation in the codebase-conventions\nartifact is self-contradicting and correctly overridden.** The\ncodebase-analysis artifact (`plan/codebase-conventions-backlog-core-decompose.md`\n\u00a71, \u00a75) recommends `_section_filter.py` / `_view_assembly.py` / `_view_budget.py`\n(leading underscore) citing `backlog_core/__init__.py`'s `_branch_delegates.py`\nas precedent, while its own \u00a71 \"Pattern 2\" section documents\n`sam_schema/__init__.py` re-exporting from `sam_schema.core.dependencies` and\n`sam_schema.core.models` \u2014 plain, non-underscored submodule names \u2014 in the\nsame document, without reconciling the conflict. I independently verified\nboth precedents by reading `backlog_core/__init__.py` and `sam_schema/__init__.py`\ndirectly: the contradiction is real, not a misreading. The team's prior\nresolution (plain names, per the task brief for this spec) is the correct\ncall \u2014 `sam_schema` is the more directly comparable precedent (both are\nmulti-module package decompositions of business logic, vs. `_branch_delegates.py`\nwhich is a single narrow delegate file for one feature). This spec uses plain\nnames throughout.\n\n**2. Six functions have zero callers outside the moving target set and are\narchitecturally orphaned if left behind \u2014 verified by grep, not asserted.**\nAcross all three phases, one or more helper functions sit physically between\nor beside the assigned target functions, are called *only* by functions that\nare moving, and are not themselves in the assigned target list:\n\n| Helper | Location (pre-move) | Sole caller(s) | Destination if moved |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `_resolve_section_indices` | operations.py:2086 | `_filter_sections`, `_apply_body_section_filter` (both Phase 1), `_assemble_view_compact` (Phase 2) | `section_filter.py` |\n| `_int_field` | operations.py:2554 | `_build_sections_compact` (Phase 1) only | `section_filter.py` |\n| `_compact_entry_count` | operations.py:2571 | `_build_sections_compact` (Phase 1) only | `section_filter.py` |\n| `_entry_owning_headers` | operations.py:2396 | `_paginate_body_result` (Phase 2) only | `view_assembly.py` |\n| `_render_paged_entry_body` | operations.py:2442 | `_paginate_body_result` (Phase 2) only | `view_assembly.py` |\n| `_sections_from_body_or_yaml` | operations.py:2766 | `_assemble_view_content` (Phase 2) only | `view_assembly.py` |\n| `_sections_index_from_result` | server.py:1928 | `_build_compact_manifest`, `_build_over_budget_view` (both Phase 3) | `view_budget.py` |\n\n(Seven rows \u2014 I initially undercounted; `_sections_index_from_result` is the\nPhase 3 instance of the same pattern, documented separately in \u00a76.4 since it\nhas a different resolution mechanics.) This spec follows the instruction not\nto re-derive module assignments and keeps all seven in their current home\nmodule, importing them back where needed (\u00a73.2, \u00a74.2, \u00a76.2/\u00a76.4) with the\nbottom-of-file placement rule (\u00a72) to keep the resulting import graph acyclic.\n**Recommendation for the team**: moving these seven into their natural\ndestination module alongside their sole caller would be a strictly smaller,\nmore cohesive result with no behavioural risk (each is a pure, self-contained\nhelper with a single call site already identified above) and would remove\nthe need for the bottom-of-file import-ordering workaround entirely. This is\na design improvement available at effectively zero marginal cost within the\nsame phase \u2014 flagging it for a decision rather than silently including it,\nsince the task brief explicitly reserved the assignment decision.\n\n**3. `_token_count` is mischaracterized as view-scoped in the item's Phase 1\nconcern-log resolution.** The item's own auto-resolved concern (2026-09-03,\n\"dh_core migration direction\") states view_budget's targets \"are MCP-response\npagination/token-budget shaping for one tool (`backlog_view`)\". That is true\nfor `_filter_view_sections`, `_build_compact_manifest`, `_build_over_budget_view`,\nand `_VIEW_TOKEN_BUDGET` \u2014 but `_token_count` itself is called from three\nplaces in `server.py` unrelated to `backlog_view`: `_view_payload_token_count`\n(221, stays in server.py, still view-scoped), a match-context sizing call at\nline 648 (search/grep result formatting \u2014 unrelated tool), and a\n`_LIST_TOKEN_BUDGET` check at line 1582 (`backlog_list` pagination \u2014 a\ndifferent tool, different budget constant). Moving `_token_count` to\n`view_budget.py` is still fine mechanically (\u00a76.3 handles it via re-export),\nbut the module's docstring should describe it accurately as \"token-counting\ninfrastructure, colocated with the view budget gate that is its primary\nconsumer\" rather than claiming it is view-exclusive \u2014 an agent reading\n`view_budget.py`'s docstring later should not be misled into thinking a\nchange to `_token_count` is safely view-scoped when two other tools depend on\nit.\n\n**4. Phase 0 ADR target path does not match either existing ADR convention\nin this repository \u2014 verified, not assumed.** The assigned path is\n`docs/adr/backlog-core-is-a-package.md` (repo-root `docs/adr/` directory).\nI searched the repository for existing ADR files and found two active\nconventions, neither of which matches: (a) `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-{N}-{n}-{slug}.md`\n\u2014 an actively used, numbered convention with at least 8 files, all within\nthe very plugin (`development-harness`) that owns `backlog_core`, e.g.\n`ADR-3075-1-content-identity-and-cache-scope.md`; (b) a single legacy flat\nfile `plugins/development-harness/docs/adr-9-close-resolve-semantics.md`.\nThere is no existing `docs/adr/` directory (singular, unnumbered) anywhere in\nthe repository \u2014 the assigned path would create a third, novel convention.\nThe task brief's stated reason for its ADR path decision only ruled out\n`.claude/rules/`; it does not appear to have checked this plugin's own\nexisting `docs/adrs/` directory. Given `backlog_core` is entirely inside\n`plugins/development-harness/`, and that directory already holds this exact\nplugin's ADR history, `plugins/development-harness/docs/adrs/ADR-2498-1-backlog-core-is-a-package.md`\nis the better-evidenced target. This spec keeps the originally assigned path\nin \u00a71 per instruction, but flags this for a decision before Phase 0 executes\n\u2014 redirecting to the established convention is a one-line change to the\nPhase 0 task's target path, not a design change.\n\n</concerns>\n"}